1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,449
Every attention-analytics product promises
to tell you

2
00:00:03,449 --> 00:00:06,260
which stretches lose your audience, and
why.

3
00:00:06,260 --> 00:00:10,460
We built that, tested the premise, and it
did not survive.

4
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:12,460
This is Second Pass.

5
00:00:12,460 --> 00:00:15,279
Not the predictor — the machine that
catches

6
00:00:15,279 --> 00:00:18,931
the predictor lying, pointed first at our
own hypothesis.

7
00:00:18,931 --> 00:00:21,731
Attention climbs toward the end of a
runtime.

8
00:00:21,731 --> 00:00:26,181
On Tears of Steel it looked like
four-point-four times the first half.

9
00:00:26,181 --> 00:00:28,511
Then we checked what was in those
segments.

10
00:00:28,511 --> 00:00:31,414
A fifth of this upload is end credits, and
the

11
00:00:31,414 --> 00:00:35,011
most replayed moment in the file is a
post-credits scene.

12
00:00:35,011 --> 00:00:37,151
On the film itself it is
one-point-two-eight.

13
00:00:37,151 --> 00:00:39,797
And the one correlation that used to clear

14
00:00:39,797 --> 00:00:43,262
our significance floor did not survive
taking them out.

15
00:00:43,262 --> 00:00:46,420
Every number you just saw came out of
ClickHouse,

16
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:50,352
through the official mcp-clickhouse MCP
server, on Cloud Run.

17
00:00:50,352 --> 00:00:51,662
The model does not write SQL.

18
00:00:51,662 --> 00:00:57,050
It picks one of seven parameterised
questions and fills in the arguments,

19
00:00:57,050 --> 00:01:02,513
so the statements stay deterministic —
which is why this demo is unedited.

20
00:01:02,513 --> 00:01:04,873
Here is what makes the rest worth
trusting.

21
00:01:04,873 --> 00:01:07,656
The same query, every feature paired

22
00:01:07,656 --> 00:01:10,979
against a different segment's attention —
a

23
00:01:10,979 --> 00:01:16,933
fixed permutation, because a control that
moves between runs is not a control.

24
00:01:16,933 --> 00:01:19,283
Plus zero-four-three-two becomes minus
zero-zero-nine.

25
00:01:19,283 --> 00:01:20,924
Nothing survives.

26
00:01:20,924 --> 00:01:22,872
Two films used to agree on

27
00:01:22,872 --> 00:01:26,994
all five features, sign test
zero-point-zero-six-three.

28
00:01:26,994 --> 00:01:31,484
That is what made us score a third.

29
00:01:31,484 --> 00:01:34,714
Corrected, those two agree on three of
five —

30
00:01:34,714 --> 00:01:37,944
the reason we kept going was credits as
well.

31
00:01:37,944 --> 00:01:40,794
Across all three: two of five, p equals
one.

32
00:01:40,794 --> 00:01:44,562
One correlation clears its floor at all,
the

33
00:01:44,562 --> 00:01:49,015
column calls it marginal, and it does not
replicate.

34
00:01:49,015 --> 00:01:55,155
The same discipline moves to a second
corpus: seventy-three real demo videos.

35
00:01:55,155 --> 00:02:00,708
Ask where a three-minute cut sits and it
gives a percentile and the range —

36
00:02:00,708 --> 00:02:06,705
eight seconds to just under nine minutes —
and refuses to call anything too long.

37
00:02:06,705 --> 00:02:09,066
A median is not a threshold.

38
00:02:09,066 --> 00:02:19,286
Three films, eighteen tests, one marginal
result that does not replicate.

39
00:02:19,286 --> 00:02:22,296
Seventy-three videos, no thresholds.

40
00:02:22,296 --> 00:02:29,416
The machine that catches the predictor
lying.

41
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:33,837
It has now caught us three times.

